Lacerations (Requiring Sutures) AUC-038
Acute Urgent Care
Full-thickness cutaneous defect, Widely gaping margins, Active haemorrhage, Exposed subcutaneous fat

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-038
Reason for Visit: Deep, actively bleeding cut from a kitchen knife requiring immediate wound closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who sustained a deep, widely gaping laceration to the forearm while chopping vegetables, with continuous venous bleeding that was difficult to control. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to achieve formal haemostasis, explore the wound for deep structural damage (tendons/nerves), and perform primary closure (suturing) within the golden window to prevent severe infection and excessive scarring, bypassing the need for an overcrowded ED presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slipped with the chef's knife, and it sliced right open; I can see the yellow fat inside and it won't stop bleeding!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a deep laceration to the left volar forearm sustained 45 minutes ago while cutting heavy vegetables. The knife slipped, causing a full-thickness cutaneous defect. The patient reports an immediate, continuous venous haemorrhage that required a tightly wrapped towel to control. The patient describes localized intense pain at the site. The wound margins are widely gaping at rest, exposing subcutaneous fat. The patient states they can still move all their fingers normally and have normal sensation in the hand.
Functional Status: Sitting at the kitchen table, holding a blood-soaked tea towel tightly against the left arm.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of ongoing blood loss and wound contamination from food preparation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is anxious but hemodynamically stable, focused on the injured arm.
Physical Exam: Removal of the pressure dressing reveals a 6cm linear, full-thickness cutaneous defect on the mid-volar aspect of the left forearm. The wound margins are cleanly incised and widely gaping. There is continuous, dark venous oozing, but no pulsatile arterial spurting. Subcutaneous fat and the superficial fascia of the flexor musculature are visible. A targeted neurovascular exam reveals intact distal capillary refill, palpable radial/ulnar pulses, and full active range of motion of the wrist and all digits against resistance, confirming no major tendinous injury. Two-point discrimination is intact distally. Expected investigations (none required) involve direct visualization of disrupted deep tissue planes without the need for advanced imaging.
Environmental Observations: Bloody cutting board and knife safely isolated on the counter; good lighting available at the kitchen table.
Medication Reconciliation: No anticoagulants. Last tetanus booster was 12 years ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lacerations (Requiring Sutures) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Arterial Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to the dark, steady venous oozing rather than bright red, pulsatile spurting synchronized with the heartbeat, and intact strong distal pulses.

Tendon Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's ability to smoothly and powerfully flex the wrist and all digits against resistance, with direct visualization showing intact underlying tendon sheaths.

Superficial Epidermal Abrasion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the widely gaping margins and direct visualization of subcutaneous adipose tissue, defining it as a full-thickness defect.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the diagnostic tetrad for a laceration requiring primary closure: a full-thickness cutaneous defect, widely gaping margins, active haemorrhage, and exposed subcutaneous fat. The thorough neurovascular exam successfully ruled out deep structural damage to tendons or major nerves. An urgent after-hours home visit was entirely appropriate to definitively irrigate and suture the wound, preventing delayed healing, significant scarring, and secondary bacterial infection, while keeping an otherwise stable patient out of a busy emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia (Lignocaine 1% with Adrenaline) infiltrating the wound edges. Irrigated the wound copiously with 500mL sterile saline. Closed the deep dermal layer with 2 interrupted absorbable sutures (Vicryl 4-0) and the epidermis with 5 interrupted non-absorbable sutures (Nylon 4-0). Haemostasis secured. Administered Adult Diphtheria-Tetanus-Pertussis (dTpa) booster IM.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping this wound completely dry for the next 48 hours and out of dirty water, you prevent environmental bacteria from tracking down the suture lines and causing a deep, painful infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Applied sterile non-adherent dressing.
Follow-up: Routine GP visit in 7-10 days for suture removal and wound check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the wound becomes increasingly red, hot, swollen, or begins to leak yellow pus over the next few days, or if you develop a fever, see your regular doctor immediately for oral antibiotics.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The wound has been cleaned and closed, but all cuts carry a small risk of infection, especially those sustained in a kitchen environment.
Clinical Course: The numb feeling from the injection will wear off in about 1-2 hours, and the area will ache. The stitches will hold the skin together while it heals over the next week.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to wash the dishes or submerge your arm in the bath. If the bandage becomes soaked with blood, apply direct, hard pressure over the top of the dressing for 15 unbroken minutes without peeking.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I used an anesthetic with adrenaline to stop the small veins from bleeding, allowing me to wash out any microscopic food particles. The stitches bring the edges perfectly together to give you the thinnest scar possible.
Actionable Steps: Keep the arm elevated on a pillow tonight to reduce the throbbing. Take simple paracetamol when the numbness wears off. Book an appointment with your clinic nurse for day 10 to have the nylon threads snipped and removed.

